# HTML & Component Rules

## Headings

- **Never use raw `<h1>`, `<h2>`, `<h3>`, `<h4>`, `<h5>` elements** — always use the `<Heading>` component from `@/components/Headings`
- The `<Heading>` component provides consistent typography, styling, and animation support
- Use `as` for the semantic HTML element and `size` for the visual size (they can differ)

```tsx
import { Heading } from "@/components/Headings"

// Page title
<Heading as="h1" size="h2">Page Title</Heading>

// Section headings
<Heading as="h2" size="h3">Section Title</Heading>

// Subsection headings
<Heading as="h3" size="h4">Subsection Title</Heading>
```

### Heading Props Reference

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
| `as` | `"h1" \| "h2" \| "h3" \| "h4" \| "h5" \| "div"` | `"h2"` | Semantic HTML element |
| `size` | `"hero" \| "h1" \| "h2" \| "h3" \| "h4" \| "h5" \| "base"` | `"h2"` | Visual size |
| `className` | `string` | — | Additional CSS class |
| `uppercase` | `boolean` | `false` | Uppercase text transform |
| `center` | `boolean` | `false` | Center-aligned text |
| `animationID` | `string` | — | Animation identifier |
| `isHidden` | `boolean` | `false` | Visually hidden |

## Buttons

- **Never use raw `<button>` elements** — always use the `<Button>` component from `@/components/Button`
- The `<Button>` component handles variants, sizes, icons, links, and accessibility

```tsx
import { Button } from "@/components/Button"

// Standard action button
<Button label="Submit" variant="primary" onClick={handleSubmit} />

// Button with icon
<Button label="Add item" variant="secondary" icon="Plus" />

// Link styled as button
<Button label="View details" variant="primary" href="/details" />

// Icon-only button (provide aria-label via label prop or wrapper)
<Button icon="X" variant="secondary" onClick={handleClose} />
```

### Button Props Reference

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
| `label` | `string` | — | Button text (omit for icon-only) |
| `variant` | `"primary" \| "secondary" \| "success" \| "danger" \| "warning" \| "toggle" \| "request" \| "link"` | `"secondary"` | Visual variant |
| `href` | `string` | — | Renders as `<Link>` when provided |
| `type` | `"button" \| "submit" \| "reset"` | `"button"` | HTML button type |
| `icon` | `string` | — | Lucide icon name (left position by default) |
| `iconAfter` | `string` | — | Lucide icon name after label |
| `iconPosition` | `"left" \| "right"` | `"left"` | Icon placement |
| `size` | `FormElementSize` | `"md"` | Button size |
| `outlined` | `boolean` | `false` | Outlined variant |
| `isRounded` | `boolean` | `false` | Rounded corners |
| `disabled` | `boolean` | `false` | Disabled state |

## Page Structure (`app/` files)

- **Pages should only contain data fetching and wrapper `<div>` elements** — all UI must be delegated to components
- Never put complex HTML structures (grids, lists, cards, forms) directly in page files
- Pages fetch data, transform it, and pass it as props to components
- Wrapper `<div>` elements for layout (e.g., applying a page-level CSS class) are acceptable

```tsx
// Good: page.tsx passes data to components
export default async function BoatPage({ params }) {
  const boat = await fetchBoat(params.id)
  return (
    <div className={styles.page}>
      <BoatHeader name={boat.name} year={boat.year} />
      <BoatSpecs specs={boat.specifications} />
      <BoatAmenities specs={boat.specifications} />
    </div>
  )
}

// Bad: page.tsx contains inline HTML structure
export default async function BoatPage({ params }) {
  const boat = await fetchBoat(params.id)
  return (
    <div>
      <div className={styles.header}>
        <h1>{boat.name}</h1>
        <div className={styles.specGrid}>
          <div className={styles.spec}>
            <span>{boat.specs.length}</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## Text & Translations

- **Never hardcode user-facing text strings** — always use `useTranslations()` from `next-intl`
- This applies to **all** visible text: labels, headings, placeholders, button text, tooltips, error messages, tab names, menu items, badges, and config arrays with display strings
- When defining config objects/arrays with labels (e.g. tab configs, menu items, filter options), store only keys/IDs and resolve labels via `t()` at render time
- All three locale files (`messages/en.json`, `messages/fr.json`, `messages/es.json`) must be updated when adding new keys

```tsx
import { useTranslations } from "next-intl"

// Good: translated text
const t = useTranslations("Booking")
<Button label={t("submit")} variant="primary" />
<Heading as="h2" size="h3">{t("title")}</Heading>
<p>{t("description")}</p>
<input placeholder={t("searchPlaceholder")} />

// Bad: hardcoded strings
<Button label="Submit" variant="primary" />
<Heading as="h2" size="h3">Booking Details</Heading>
<p>Select your preferred date</p>
```

- For strings with dynamic values, use ICU message format in the JSON files and pass parameters:

```tsx
// messages/en.json: { "Booking": { "pricePerDay": "From {price} per day" } }
t("pricePerDay", { price: boat.price })
```

## Form Inputs

- **Never use raw `<input>` elements** — always use the `<Input>` component from `@/components/Forms`
- **Never use raw `<select>` elements** — always use the `<Select>` component from `@/components/Forms`
- Both components support a `label` prop — use it instead of rendering a separate `<label>` element
- Use the `full` prop to make the field fill its container width
- Use the `id` prop for explicit label association (the component handles `htmlFor` internally)

```tsx
import { Input, Select } from "@/components/Forms"

// Text input with label
<Input type="text" id="name" label={t("nameLabel")} placeholder={t("namePlaceholder")} full />

// Number input with label
<Input type="number" id="price" label={t("priceLabel")} value={price} onChange={handleChange} full />

// Select with label
<Select id="category" label={t("categoryLabel")} value={category} onChange={handleChange} full>
  <option value="a">Option A</option>
  <option value="b">Option B</option>
</Select>

// Bad: raw elements and external labels
<label htmlFor="name">Name</label>
<input type="text" id="name" />

<label htmlFor="category">Category</label>
<select id="category">
  <option value="a">Option A</option>
</select>
```

## Semantic HTML

- Use `<main>` for the primary content area (already in layout.tsx)
- Use `<section>` for distinct content sections, prefer the `<Section>` component from `@/components/Section`
- Use `<nav>` for navigation regions
- Use `<Button>` for actions, `<Link>` for navigation — never the reverse
- **Never wrap a block-level component with an inline element** like `<a>` or `<Link>` — if a card needs to be clickable, pass `link` as a prop instead of wrapping it
- **Always use the `<Modal>` component from `@/components/Modal` for modals, overlays, and confirmation dialogs** — never build dialogs from `<div>` overlays with `onClick` handlers. `<Modal>` wraps the native `<dialog>` element and provides `showModal()`/`close()`, `::backdrop`, Escape key, and outside-click handling out of the box
- All interactive elements must have accessible names (`aria-label`, visible text, or `aria-labelledby`)

## Storybook

- Every reusable component in `components/` must have a colocated `.stories.tsx` file
- Stories use `@storybook/nextjs` framework with `Meta` and `StoryObj` types
- Provide meaningful mock data in story args to demonstrate component states
